  :body
  {:problem-class
   "Prove absolute continuity on `[0,1]` from continuity and bounded variation on `[0,1]` plus absolute continuity on every truncated interval `[a,1]` with `a > 0`."
   :primitive-construction
   ["Use `BoundedVariationOn.intervalIntegrable_deriv` to show that `deriv f` is interval-integrable on `[0,1]`."
    "Define `g x = f 0 + ∫ t in 0..x, deriv f t`."
    "Use `IntervalIntegrable.absolutelyContinuousOnInterval_intervalIntegral` and absolute continuity of constants to prove that `g` is absolutely continuous on `[0,1]`."]
   :endpoint-identification
   ["Fix `x ∈ (0,1]` and set `a n = x/n`, so `a n → 0` through `[0,1]`."
    "For sufficiently large `n`, restrict the hypothesis on `[a n,1]` to `[a n,x]`."
    "Apply `AbsolutelyContinuousOnInterval.integral_deriv_eq_sub` on `[a n,x]`."
    "Split the primitive integral at `a n`; the local FTC identity makes `f (a n) - g (a n)` equal to the constant `f x - g x` eventually."
    "Continuity of `f` and absolute continuity of `g` make the same difference tend to `f 0 - g 0 = 0` as `a n → 0`."
    "Use uniqueness of limits to conclude `f x = g x`; the endpoint `x = 0` follows directly from the definition of `g`."]
   :transfer
   "Rewrite the epsilon-delta definition of absolute continuity for `f` by the pointwise equality `f = g` on `[0,1]` and apply absolute continuity of `g`."
